WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:02.601
-Gugu, hello, nice to meet you.

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:03.434
Thank you for
coming on the show.

00:00:03.434 --> 00:00:04.501
-Thank you.

00:00:04.501 --> 00:00:06.033
-I know we're meeting
through Zoom.

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:09.701
It's odd, but I bet you a lot of
people are meeting through Zoom.

00:00:09.701 --> 00:00:11.634
-I know. It's crazy.

00:00:11.634 --> 00:00:13.934
This is it.
This is how we live now.

00:00:13.934 --> 00:00:15.667
-Yeah. This is it.

00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:18.434
I was going to ask if you're
in London,

00:00:18.434 --> 00:00:20.334
but you're in Atlanta right now,
right?

00:00:20.334 --> 00:00:23.367
-I'm in Atlanta right now, yeah.
I'm here.

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:28.467
I just restarted filming here
on "Loki",

00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:29.934
the show that I'm working on.

00:00:29.934 --> 00:00:33.901
-Oh, "Loki," for Disney+.
-Yeah.

00:00:33.901 --> 00:00:38.467
-Disney Plu!
-Disney Plu! Yeah!

00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:42.234
-No one calls it that,
but we can start that today.

00:00:42.234 --> 00:00:44.133
-There we go.
It's going to be a thing.

00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:46.901
It's going to be a thing.
-Were you a big Marvel fan

00:00:46.901 --> 00:00:49.400
before joining the show?
-You know,

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.567
I wasn't a massive fan.

00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:53.634
I have to confess,
I wasn't a huge fan,

00:00:53.634 --> 00:00:56.734
but I did sort of binge
all the shows before

00:00:56.734 --> 00:00:58.501
starting this because,
obviously,

00:00:58.501 --> 00:01:01.033
it's inspired by a character
from "Thor"

00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:02.467
and there's all
of the "Thor" movies,

00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:06.267
so I got Disney Plu,
and I sort of binged

00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:09.701
on everything and caught up.
So now I know what I'm doing.

00:01:09.701 --> 00:01:14.067
-Many people have taken up
hobbies while in quarantine,

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:18.334
and you posted these portraits
that you painted on Instagram,

00:01:18.334 --> 00:01:22.133
and they're gorgeous.
You went from painting people

00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:24.634
you know to painting portraits
of the George Floyd

00:01:24.634 --> 00:01:27.234
and Breonna Taylor
and John Lewis.

00:01:27.234 --> 00:01:29.801
-Yeah.
-What inspired you

00:01:29.801 --> 00:01:33.834
to paint their portraits?
-Well, you know, I think,

00:01:33.834 --> 00:01:39.267
like everyone, I just found
the shifts that were happening

00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:42.734
culturally, you know,
post the murder of George Floyd

00:01:42.734 --> 00:01:46.167
and all of the protests that
ensued, and I was painting

00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:48.701
every day anyway,
and it was just such a shock,

00:01:48.701 --> 00:01:51.434
and just such an emotional time.

00:01:51.434 --> 00:01:56.300
Somehow, just being able to
channel those emotions into art

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.434
just was sort of a way for me
to process it,

00:01:59.434 --> 00:02:01.634
I think, at the time.
And then, you know,

00:02:01.634 --> 00:02:03.734
just to be able to paint
Breonna Taylor,

00:02:03.734 --> 00:02:07.334
actually took that painting
on a protest with me in L.A.

00:02:07.334 --> 00:02:11.400
as a banner, and I wrote
"Say Her Name" on the back,

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:17.234
and was able to actually auction
those two paintings for charity,

00:02:17.234 --> 00:02:20.367
for some social justice
organizations as well.

00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:21.667
-See?
-Yeah,

00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:24.601
it's took on a greater purpose.

00:02:24.601 --> 00:02:28.400
-I read that when you're
working as an actor

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.934
that you use music to help you
prepare for roles.

00:02:30.934 --> 00:02:33.601
Are you musical?

00:02:33.601 --> 00:02:36.467
-You know, I like to sing,

00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:38.434
and I did grow up
playing the saxophone,

00:02:38.434 --> 00:02:42.868
which was really a treat for me.
I haven't played it for a while.

00:02:42.868 --> 00:02:46.267
-Oh, I love the saxophone.
That's so tricky.

00:02:46.267 --> 00:02:47.934
What made you get into
saxophone?

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:51.601
-Oh, my God. Well, I think,
probably, Lisa Simpson

00:02:51.601 --> 00:02:54.934
had a little bit to do with it.
-For real?

00:02:54.934 --> 00:02:57.367
-Yeah, for real.
Just watching that show

00:02:57.367 --> 00:03:00.767
And just seeing a young girl
just playing the saxophone,

00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:03.467
I was like, that is so cool.
I want to do that.

00:03:03.467 --> 00:03:08.334
It's possible. And I just was
so inspired by her.

00:03:08.334 --> 00:03:09.968
-Wow!
-Yeah.

00:03:09.968 --> 00:03:12.701
And I grew up listening
to all the jazz divas,

00:03:12.701 --> 00:03:16.267
the greats like Ella Fitzgerald
and Billie Holiday,

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:20.334
and I never really saw
any female saxophonists.

00:03:20.334 --> 00:03:24.701
There's Coltrane and all these
other famous jazz people,

00:03:24.701 --> 00:03:27.801
but you know, Lisa Simpson
for me was the one

00:03:27.801 --> 00:03:29.734
that made me think, "Girls
can do it, too," you know?

00:03:29.734 --> 00:03:31.834
-That makes -- that's so cool.

00:03:31.834 --> 00:03:34.400
I want to talk about your
new movie "Misbehaviour."

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.834
This is based on a true story
of what happened

00:03:37.834 --> 00:03:41.300
at the Miss World competition
in 1970.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.434
A lot of people might not know
the story.

00:03:43.434 --> 00:03:45.267
I wasn't that familiar
with the story.

00:03:45.267 --> 00:03:46.901
Can you tell everyone
what it's about?

00:03:46.901 --> 00:03:49.300
-Yeah, I didn't know
anything about it

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:50.734
until I read the script,
and it's kind of

00:03:50.734 --> 00:03:55.100
an amazing story because,
you know, really, in 1970,

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:59.300
it was around the period that
the women's liberation movement

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:03.033
were forming,
and the ceremony itself

00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:06.434
was the most watched TV show
in the world.

00:04:06.434 --> 00:04:08.868
This particular year in 1970,

00:04:08.868 --> 00:04:12.167
the women's liberation movement
stormed the ceremony

00:04:12.167 --> 00:04:15.200
in the middle for the feminist
cause and they threw

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.834
flower bombs on Bob Hope,
completely disrupted

00:04:17.834 --> 00:04:20.234
the whole ceremony,
but it was also the year

00:04:20.234 --> 00:04:24.133
that the first woman of color,
Miss Grenada, who I play,

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:25.968
won the competition.

00:04:25.968 --> 00:04:29.367
So it's kind of this amazing
intersection of events

00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:31.534
between sort of, you know,
the birth of feminism

00:04:31.534 --> 00:04:33.334
in the '70s on one hand,

00:04:33.334 --> 00:04:35.667
and then this huge step
forward in terms

00:04:35.667 --> 00:04:37.868
of representation
for women of color

00:04:37.868 --> 00:04:40.434
and perceptions of beauty
on the other.

00:04:40.434 --> 00:04:42.834
So, yeah,
it was an amazing year,

00:04:42.834 --> 00:04:47.167
and, you know, it's obviously
the '70s fashions and the music

00:04:47.167 --> 00:04:49.300
and the era is just really,
really fun

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.834
to look back on as well.
-I want to show a clip.

00:04:51.834 --> 00:04:56.901
Here is Gugu Mbatha-Raw in
"Misbehaviour." Take a look.

00:04:56.901 --> 00:05:00.267
-There will be little girls
watching tonight

00:05:00.267 --> 00:05:06.701
who will see themselves
differently because I won,

00:05:06.701 --> 00:05:08.234
who might just start to believe

00:05:08.234 --> 00:05:14.200
that you don't have to be white
to have a place in the world.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.934
-I'm glad.

00:05:16.934 --> 00:05:20.334
I -- I really hope
that the world opens up

00:05:20.334 --> 00:05:24.734
for them and for you,

00:05:24.734 --> 00:05:30.000
but making us compete with
each other over the way we look,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.701
doesn't make the world narrower
for all of us in the end?

00:05:33.701 --> 00:05:39.801
-[ Sighs ]

00:05:39.801 --> 00:05:42.834
What's your name?

00:05:42.834 --> 00:05:45.033
-Sally.

00:05:45.033 --> 00:05:48.501
-Well, Sally,

00:05:48.501 --> 00:05:51.067
all I can say is

00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:54.100
I look forward to having
your choices in life.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.400
-Gugu Mbatha-Raw, everybody.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.567
"Misbehaviour" is available
on all major digital

00:05:58.567 --> 00:06:00.634
and cable platforms today.

